---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Anim from "../../components/Anim.astro";
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="@open-rgs/holdwin, Respins"
  description="Hold and win: a landing resets the counter. Where coins come from each respin, how a mystery coin resolves, and where a multiplier lives."
>
  <main>
    <p class="dim"><a href="/extension">All extensions</a></p>
    <h1>@open-rgs/holdwin</h1>

    <p class="lede">A landing resets the counter.</p>

    <h2 data-section="&sect; 1">The cycle</h2>

    <p>N coins on a base board trigger respins. Coins lock, and every new coin resets the counter rather than decrementing it. That single rule is what makes the feature feel alive, and it is why a cycle has no natural end: the max-win cap is what makes the round finite.</p>

    <Anim kind="holdwin-meter" caption="Respins burn down, then a coin lands and the counter snaps back to full." />

    <Code code={`import { triggers, beginRespins, stepRespins, isCycleOver, settleRespins } from "@open-rgs/holdwin";

const CONFIG = { respins: 3, fullBoardAward: "GRAND" };

if (triggers(grid, 6)) {
  let s = beginRespins(grid, CONFIG);
  while (!isCycleOver(s)) s = stepRespins(s, landed, CONFIG);
}`} />

    <h2 data-section="&sect; 2">Where the coins come from</h2>

    <p>Nothing in this package generates a landing. <code>stepRespins</code> takes the coins that landed and applies them; drawing them is your math's job, and it is where the feature's whole distribution lives. Two lines decide the game: which cells get a chance, and what a coin is worth when it takes.</p>

    <p>Only empty cells are considered. A locked coin never moves, so a landing aimed at an occupied cell is discarded rather than overwriting it, and <code>emptyPositions</code> is the list to iterate.</p>

    <Anim kind="holdwin-land" caption="Every empty cell gets its own chance each respin. Locked coins sit still." />

    <Code code={`import { emptyPositions } from "@open-rgs/holdwin";

/** One independent trial per empty cell: the usual shape. */
function land(grid, next) {
  const out = [];
  for (const pos of emptyPositions(grid)) {
    if (next() < LAND_CHANCE) out.push([pos, COINS.pick(next())]);
  }
  return out;
}

state = stepRespins(state, land(state.grid, host.rng_next), CONFIG);`} />

    <p>The alternative is to draw a <em>count</em> first and then place that many coins. Both are legitimate and they are not the same game. Per-cell trials make landings scale with how empty the board is, so a nearly full board almost never lands again and the cycle ends; a fixed count keeps landing at the same rate until there is nowhere left to put a coin, which pushes far more cycles to a full board. Pick one deliberately, because the full-board award is usually the largest single term in the feature's RTP.</p>

    <p>The counter follows from the landings, not from the values: any coin at all resets it. That is what makes <code>LAND_CHANCE</code> the tuning dial for cycle length, and the coin set the dial for what a cycle pays.</p>

    <h2 data-section="&sect; 3">Five mechanics, one shape</h2>

    <p>Collector, payer, multiplier, upgrader and spawner are the same mechanism with different selectors: a source cell, a set of target cells, and an operation. Adding &quot;collect only from the same column&quot; is a selector, not a new engine.</p>

    <Anim kind="holdwin-collect" caption="A collector absorbs the value of every coin it targets." />

    <Code code={`import { collector, payer, upgrader, cashCells, tierCells } from "@open-rgs/holdwin";

collector(cashCells(), JACKPOTS);   // absorb every cash coin, leave jackpots
upgrader(tierCells(["MINI"]));      // bump one rung up the ladder
payer(5, coinCells());              // add 5x to every coin on the board`} />

    <p>Each returns an <code>Effect</code>: <code>(grid, source, next) =&gt; grid</code>. Your math decides <em>when</em> to run one, and that timing is a design decision rather than a detail. A collector that fires the moment it lands harvests a small board; one that fires at the end of the cycle harvests everything. Same effect, different game.</p>

    <h2 data-section="&sect; 4">Mystery coins</h2>

    <p>A mystery coin lands face down and is worth something only once it turns over, usually when the cycle ends. Model it as a placeholder: land a marker coin, keep it on the board like any other so it locks its cell and resets the counter, and write real values in at reveal.</p>

    <Anim kind="holdwin-mystery" caption="Hidden cells resolve together, to one value." />

    <Code code={`import { positionsWhere, withAt } from "@open-rgs/grid";

/** A marker, recognised by identity rather than by value. */
const MYSTERY = { value: 0 };

function landWithMystery(grid, next) {
  const out = [];
  for (const pos of emptyPositions(grid)) {
    if (next() >= LAND_CHANCE) continue;
    out.push([pos, next() < MYSTERY_SHARE ? MYSTERY : COINS.pick(next())]);
  }
  return out;
}

/** Classic reveal: every hidden cell turns over to the SAME value. */
function reveal(grid, next) {
  const hidden = positionsWhere(grid, (c) => c === MYSTERY);
  if (hidden.length === 0) return grid;
  const value = COINS.pick(next());
  return withAt(grid, hidden.map((p) => [p, value]));
}`} />

    <p><strong>One value or one each.</strong> Revealing every hidden cell to the same value is the genre convention, and it is not a cosmetic choice. With four hidden cells and the same coin set, sharing one value pays the same on average as drawing four independently and swings twice as hard: measured over 200,000 reveals, mean 10.10 against 10.11, standard deviation 14.04 against 7.01. Shared reveals are how a mystery feature produces its screenshot moments without moving RTP.</p>

    <p><strong>Where the draw actually happens.</strong> Drawing the value at landing and hiding it in the client is equivalent to drawing it at reveal, with one condition: nothing between the landing and the reveal may read the value. The moment a collector, payer or multiplier runs over the board, that stops being true.</p>

    <p><strong>The ordering trap.</strong> A collector that fires before the reveal harvests every mystery cell at its placeholder value, which is zero. In a five-by-three board with three hidden cells, the same collector took 45 before the reveal and 69 after. Reveal first, then run the effects, unless your paytable is priced for the other order.</p>

    <p>One caveat on the marker: <code>EMPTIED</code>, the coin a collector leaves behind, is also worth zero. Recognise a mystery by identity (<code>c === MYSTERY</code>) or with your own set of positions, never by testing <code>value === 0</code>.</p>

    <h2 data-section="&sect; 5">Multipliers on coins, multipliers on cells</h2>

    <p>Two different things share the name. A multiplier <em>coin</em> is an effect that scales other coins, and it ships here. A multiplier printed on a <em>cell</em> belongs to the board rather than to any coin, so it lives in your math as a map from cell to factor, and the library never sees it.</p>

    <Anim kind="holdwin-cellmult" caption="The factor belongs to the cell. Whatever lands there is worth more." />

    <Code code={`import { multiplier, coinCells, valueOf } from "@open-rgs/holdwin";

// on a coin: this coin scales every other coin on the board
const x3 = multiplier(3, coinCells());

// on a cell: the board carries the factor, by flat grid index
const CELL_MULT = new Map([[4, 2], [7, 3]]);

function settleWithCellMultipliers(grid, jackpots) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < grid.cells.length; i++) {
    const c = grid.cells[i];
    if (c) total += valueOf(c, jackpots) * (CELL_MULT.get(i) ?? 1);
  }
  return total;
}`} />

    <p>Three rules keep either kind priced.</p>

    <p><strong>Tiers are left alone.</strong> <code>multiplier</code> and <code>payer</code> skip a coin carrying a tier, because a &quot;2x GRAND&quot; is not a rung on the ladder and a jackpot that is sometimes worth double is a different jackpot. Scale cash; upgrade tiers.</p>

    <p><strong>Apply once, at a point you can name.</strong> A cell factor applied at settle multiplies whatever finished there. Applied at landing instead, it multiplies the coin that landed and then anything the coin later collects rides on it too. The second is a much larger number, and both are shipped games, so write down which one you built.</p>

    <p><strong>Multipliers compose with the collector, hard.</strong> A collector that merges a board into one cell, standing on a 3x cell, triples the merged total rather than tripling one coin. That is the interaction that produces max-win boards, so it is the one to simulate before it ships, and the reason the round needs <code>maxWinMultiplier</code> whatever the paytable says.</p>

    <h2 data-section="&sect; 6">Tiers resolve through one table</h2>

    <p>A jackpot coin carries a tier, not a number, so retuning GRAND never means touching every coin. A collected coin is emptied rather than removed: it still fills the board, and it cannot be harvested twice.</p>

    <Anim kind="holdwin-full" caption="Filling the board pays the top tier." />

    <Code code={`import { mixedCoinSet, coin, settleRespins } from "@open-rgs/holdwin";

const JACKPOTS = { MINI: 8, MINOR: 20, MAJOR: 60, GRAND: 400 };

const COINS = mixedCoinSet(
  [{ item: coin(1), weight: 46 }, { item: coin(5), weight: 8 }],
  { MINI: 5, MINOR: 1.6, MAJOR: 0.35 },
);

settleRespins(state, CONFIG, JACKPOTS);`} />

    <p>Collecting a tier coin needs the table, so <code>collector(targets, JACKPOTS)</code> throws without it rather than absorbing a GRAND at nothing.</p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
